Write finding notes for session {{ session_id }}

Look for issues in session {{ session_id }}. An issue is something wrong or
costly that the session gives evidence of that the user would likely want to
address after the session.

Examples (non-exhaustive):

- A tool or integration that misbehaved
- Friction the user faced
- Assistant misbehaving or asserting something wrong without correction
- A question or issue the user raised that was left unaddressed
- Concerns or important questions mentioned in assistant thinking blocks that
  the assistant did not address or surface to the user

The evidence must be in the record itself. Do not infer or assume conditions
beyond what the record shows.

Note that sessions exist to raise and address issues. Sessions therefore
normally contains errors, bugs, and confusion. Do not report issues that the
session itself diagnoses, fixes, or answers.

Many sessions do not contain issues. Do not force issues onto a session.

Read the conversation by querying the `message` table via `mcp__gage__Query`,
filtering by `session_id = '{{ session_id }}'`. `message` table columns:
(session_id, line, uuid, type, subtype, text, timestamp, attachments, ide_tags,
raw)

Write found issues using the `mcp__gage__Finding` tool using a single request
rather than one tool use per finding. This saves time and tokens.

If you did not find issues for the session, do not use `mcp__gage__Finding` to
report "no issues found". Only use the tool to report found issues.

In your reply, simply state whether you wrote findings. Do not re-state the findings in your reply.

## User interrupts

User interrupts are sometimes useful for identifying issues. A user interrupt
occurs when the user presses the Escape key during an assistant turn. This may
indicate that the user is unhappy with something. It may indicate the user wants
to revise their last message (e.g. mis-typed, missing content, etc.). Not all
user interrupts indicate underlying issues worth reporting. Use your best
judgement.

## Tool errors

A tool use error may imply that the assistant has bad instructions or has made a
mistake. It may imply that the tool itself has issues.

Note that it's common to see mechanical file-state feedback from the Edit and
Write tools: "File has not been read yet", "file has been modified, re-read it",
"String to replace not found in file". These are part of the normal edit cycle
and normally do not imply issues. A tool error is evidence of an issue when it
repeats without recovery, blocks the session, reveals a defect/bug in the tool, or
is a result of incorrect or ambiguous tool documentation.
